= Qing Dai (Indigo Naturalis, 青黛)

_Scope: this entry treats *青黛 (qīng dài)*, the dried blue-black pigment precipitated from indigo-bearing leaves by lime-water fermentation — pharmacy name *Indigo Naturalis*. Note what the drug *is*: not a plant part but a *processed precipitate*, so the processing is constitutive and the entry treats it as a manufactured substance in the way ghee or jaggery are treated, not as a herb. The Chinese Pharmacopoeia admits three source plants: *馬藍 (Baphicacanthus / Strobilanthes cusia*, Acanthaceae), *蓼藍 (Polygonum tinctorium*, Polygonaceae — the Japanese dye plant), and *菘藍 (Isatis indigotica*, Brassicaceae — woad). The first and third also yield the root drug *板藍根* and the leaf drug *大青葉*, so one plant can give three separate canonical drugs; see ban-lan-gen.typ, which this entry extends rather than repeats. Status: classical in Chinese medicine, but *late* — a Tang–Song entrant, absent from the Shennong Bencao Jing. A crucial practical point governs its use: *青黛 is water-insoluble and is not decocted*; it is given as powder in pills and capsules, or applied externally. Indigo the pigment is separately classical in Ayurveda (नीली) and in Tibb (نيل), but from a *different* source plant, Indigofera tinctoria — same molecule, independent traditions, and in Ayurveda's case an apparently opposite thermal reading. Modern register: unusually consequential — indirubin from this drug produced a real anticancer agent, and indigo naturalis for ulcerative colitis has both good trial evidence and a serious pulmonary-hypertension safety signal._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin / pharmacy:* *_Indigo Naturalis_*; older European pharmacy *_Indigo pulverata levis_*. The drug is the *dried foam-precipitate*, not a plant part.

*Source plants (three, per the modern Chinese Pharmacopoeia):*
- *_Baphicacanthus cusia_ (Nees) Bremek.* (= *_Strobilanthes cusia_*), Acanthaceae — *馬藍 (mǎlán)*; the principal southern-Chinese source.
- *_Polygonum tinctorium_ Ait.* (= *_Persicaria tinctoria_*), Polygonaceae — *蓼藍 (liǎolán)*; the dyer's knotweed, and the plant of the Japanese indigo tradition.
- *_Isatis indigotica_ Fort.* (= *_I. tinctoria_* L.), Brassicaceae — *菘藍 (sōnglán)*; *woad*, the European dye plant.
Three families, one pigment: as `ban-lan-gen.typ` sets out for the first and third, these unrelated plants converge on the same chemistry, elaborating *indican*, which on crushing and fermentation releases *indoxyl*, oxidizing to blue *indigotin* and to its red isomer *indirubin*. *A note on scope relative to the sibling entry:* ban-lan-gen treats two source plants because the *root* drug comes from two; 青黛 admits three, the Japanese knotweed being a pigment source but not a root drug.#footnote[Source-plant list: _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ (current edition), 青黛 monograph, which admits _Baphicacanthus cusia_, _Polygonum tinctorium_, and _Isatis indigotica_. The indican → indoxyl → indigotin/indirubin pathway and the convergence across Acanthaceae, Polygonaceae and Brassicaceae: standard phytochemical literature; see `ban-lan-gen.typ` for the fuller treatment of the convergence.]

*Chinese:* *青黛 (qīng dài)* — and the name is a *cosmetic* one, which is the entry's best etymological point. *青 (qīng)* is the famously unstable Chinese colour word spanning blue, green, and blue-black. *黛 (dài)* is not a general colour term at all: it names the *blue-black mineral-or-vegetable pigment with which women painted their eyebrows*, and it is overwhelmingly a word of cosmetics and poetry — *黛眉* (painted brows), *粉黛* ("powder and brow-black," a metonym for women or for a harem). So 青黛 reads as "blue-black brow-pigment," and the drug's name records that the same substance sat on the dressing table and in the dispensary. Classical Bencao authors note the cosmetic use directly.#footnote[黛 as the eyebrow-pigment word and its poetic register (黛眉, 粉黛): _Hanyu Da Cidian_; the term is pervasive in Tang and Song verse. The cosmetic use of indigo-class pigments for eyebrow painting is recorded in the Bencao tradition and in Chinese cosmetic history. The 青 colour-range problem (blue/green/black) is a standard point in Chinese lexicography.]

*Other Chinese names:* *靛花 (diànhuā,* "indigo flower") — the *froth* skimmed from the fermentation vat, which is the finest grade and strictly what 青黛 should be; *藍靛 (lándiàn)*, *靛沫 (diànmò)*. *青蛤粉* is a *compounded* product (with clam-shell powder), not a synonym — see Formulas.

*Japanese:* *青黛 (seitai)* the drug; *藍 (ai)* the plant and the colour; *藍染 (aizome)* indigo dyeing; *すくも (sukumo)*, the composted leaf-mass of the Tokushima *阿波藍 (awa-ai)* tradition. The phrase *Japan blue* (coined by a 19th-c. Western visitor for the ubiquity of indigo-dyed cloth) belongs to this register. *Korean:* *청대 (cheongdae)*.

*Sanskrit:* *नीली (nīlī)* / *नीलिनी (nīlinī)*, from *नील (nīla,* "dark blue, black"). *This is a genuine classical Ayurvedic drug — but a different plant*: Indian *nīlī* is *_Indigofera tinctoria_* L. (Fabaceae), the legume that supplied most of the world's indigo trade. Same pigment, unrelated plant, independent tradition. See the Ayurveda section.

*Arabic / Persian (Tibb):* *نِيل (nīl)*, *نِيلَج (nīlaj)*; Persian *نیل (nīl)*. *The Arabic word is a borrowing of the Sanskrit* नील *(nīla)*, carried west with the dye itself.

*English — and a double etymology worth setting out:* the two great names for this substance travelled by *two independent routes*.
- *Indigo* comes from provenance: Greek *ἰνδικόν (indikón,* "the Indian [dye]") → Latin *indicum* → Spanish/Portuguese *índigo* → English. The word says *where it came from*.
- *Nīl / nīlī / anil* comes from colour: Sanskrit *नील (nīla,* "dark blue") → Arabic *نيل* → Spanish and Portuguese *añil / anil* (still the ordinary Portuguese word for indigo) → the chemical name *aniline*, coined in the 19th c. from the Portuguese.
So the same substance named itself to the West twice — once by its origin and once by its colour — and the second route is why the foundational dye-chemistry word *aniline* is, at root, Sanskrit for "blue."#footnote[Etymologies: Liddell & Scott (s.v. ἰνδικόν); Lewis & Short (s.v. _indicum_); _Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "indigo", "anil", "aniline"); Monier-Williams (s.v. नील, नीली). The _anil_ → _aniline_ derivation (via Portuguese, from Arabic from Sanskrit) is the standard account of the chemical term's origin.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

*Status and date — flagged, because it matters.* 青黛 is *classical but late*: it is *not* in the *神農本草經*, and enters the Bencao tradition in the *Tang–Song* period, conventionally credited to the *藥性論 (Yàoxìng Lùn)* and the Song *開寶本草 (Kāibǎo Běncǎo,* 973). Li Shizhen gives it a substantial *本草綱目* entry, discussing both its medical and cosmetic uses and the manufacture. *The specific first attestation should be verified against a particular edition*; what is secure is that it postdates the Han canon by many centuries, which is unsurprising for a drug that depends on a fermentation technology.#footnote[青黛's Tang–Song entry into the Bencao tradition (commonly credited to _Yàoxìng Lùn_ and _Kāibǎo Běncǎo_, 973) and its absence from the _Shennong Bencao Jing_: standard Bencao-historical literature; Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, 青黛 entry. The precise earliest attestation is flagged for verification against a specific edition.]

*Property (性 xìng):* Cold (寒) — and by most accounts *markedly* so.

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Salty (鹹); many sources add bitter (苦). *The salty attribution is the distinctive one* and is somewhat unusual for a heat-clearing plant-derived drug — it reflects the *lime-processed, mineral-like* character of the finished pigment rather than the taste of the source leaf, which is a nice instance of processing changing a drug's classical flavour attribution.

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Liver (肝), Lung (肺), Stomach (胃). *The Liver entry is what distinguishes it* from the sibling drugs: `ban-lan-gen.typ` gives 板藍根 as Heart and Stomach, and the root is not a Liver drug. 青黛's Liver scope underwrites its two most characteristic uses — Liver-fire cough and childhood convulsions.

*Actions and indications:*
- *清熱解毒 (qīng rè jiě dú)* — clears heat and resolves toxicity: the family action shared with 板藍根 and 大青葉; for sores, mouth and tongue ulcers (口瘡), sore swollen throat, and mumps (痄腮), internally and topically.
- *涼血消斑 (liáng xuè xiāo bān)* — cools blood and disperses *maculae*: for *溫毒發斑* (warm-toxin with macular eruption) and *血熱吐衄* (blood-heat with vomiting of blood or nosebleed). 斑 (bān) are the flat, non-raised purple-red skin eruptions of heat forced into the blood level — a Wen Bing category (see `climates.typ` for the warm-disease frame).
- *清肝瀉火 (qīng gān xiè huǒ)* — clears Liver fire, and specifically the pattern *肝火犯肺* ("Liver fire assailing the Lung"): a harsh, paroxysmal cough with scanty sticky or *blood-streaked* sputum, chest and rib-side pain, irritability, a red tongue with yellow coat and a wiry rapid pulse. The formula for this is *黛蛤散* (below), and it is 青黛's signature indication.
- *定驚 / 息風 (dìng jīng)* — settles fright and convulsion: for *小兒驚癇* (childhood convulsive disorders and fright-epilepsy) with high fever — a Liver-channel action, the Liver governing wind.

*Cautions (classical):* the drug is *cold and draining*; it is contraindicated in *胃寒* (stomach cold) and in Spleen-Stomach vacuity-cold, where it causes epigastric discomfort, nausea, and loose stools. Classical sources advise against prolonged internal use.

*The practical rule that governs all internal use — 青黛 is not decocted.* The pigment is *water-insoluble*: boiling it in a decoction extracts essentially nothing and wastes the drug. It is therefore given as *powder, in pills or capsules* (入丸散), or stirred into a prepared decoction after straining, or — in the modern idiom — taken in capsules. Where a formula does call for it in a decoction it must be *包煎* (wrapped in cloth) or, more usually, *沖服* (stirred in). *This is a practical point that distinguishes it from almost every other heat-clearing drug and is easy to get wrong.*#footnote[Properties, channels, actions, and the 肝火犯肺 indication: Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ (current edition), 青黛 monograph. The water-insolubility and the resulting 入丸散 / 沖服 administration rule are standard pharmacy practice and are stated in the Pharmacopoeia and in standard materia medica references. The salty-flavour attribution reflecting lime processing is noted as an observation about the classical attribution, not a claim from a specific text.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_A middle case, tiered honestly: *indigo* is a classical Ayurvedic drug, but *this* indigo is not. The Sanskrit* नीली *(nīlī) names* Indigofera tinctoria *L. (Fabaceae) — a legume of the Indian subcontinent, botanically unrelated to the three Chinese source plants and to European woad, though it yields the same indigotin. The classical dravyaguna attributions below belong to that plant; the Chinese 青黛 as a prepared drug has no Ayurvedic entry. Treating these as one drug would be a category error, and treating them as unrelated would miss that the active pigment is identical._

*Rasa:* Tikta (तिक्त, bitter), Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent).

*Guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry), Tīkṣṇa (तीक्ष्ण, sharp).

*Vīrya:* *Uṣṇa (उष्ण, heating)* in the attributions commonly given for नीली — *and if that is right, it is a head-on cross-system divergence worth stopping on*: the Chinese reading of the same pigment is emphatically *cold* (寒), used to clear heat and cool blood, while the Indian reading of indigo-the-drug is warming, used against kṛmi (worms), viṣa (poison), and obstinate skin disease. Two traditions, one molecule, opposite thermal placements. *Flagged for verification:* I have not confirmed the vīrya against a specific Bhāvaprakāśa or Rāja Nighaṇṭu edition, and since the whole divergence claim rests on it, it should be checked before being relied on.

*Vipāka:* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).

*Prabhāva:* *None classically named* for नीली in the sources consulted. If one were proposed, the candidate would be its *keśya* (hair-blackening) action — a pigmentary effect on the body's own colouring that is not derivable from tikta-kaṣāya rasa or uṣṇa vīrya and is, in a plain sense, the dye acting as a dye. Flagged as this book's suggestion, not a classical attribution.

*Doṣa effect:* Kapha-hara and Vāta-hara (the uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa-rūkṣa profile); Pitta-vardhaka in excess.

*Karma:* _Kṛmighna_ (anthelminthic — a prominent classical use), _kuṣṭhaghna_ (against the broad kuṣṭha class of skin diseases), _viṣaghna_ (anti-poison), _keśya_ (hair-benefiting and hair-blackening), _plīhaghna_ (spleen-disorder-relieving), _dīpana_; used in _udara_ (abdominal disorders) and _kāsa-śvāsa_.

*The living preparation:* *नीलीभृङ्गादि तैल (Nīlībhṛṅgādi taila)* — a classical medicated hair oil in which नीली is a named ingredient alongside *भृङ्गराज (bhṛṅgarāja)*, used for hair loss and premature greying. It remains in manufacture, which makes it the most tangible point of contact between the Ayurvedic indigo register and current practice. *Indigo powder with henna* for hair dyeing is a widespread modern Indian practice, continuous with the classical _keśya_ register but cosmetic rather than medical.#footnote[नीली as _Indigofera tinctoria_: Monier-Williams (s.v. नीली, नीलिनी); standard dravyaguna references (P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_) and the nighaṇṭu tradition (Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, Rāja Nighaṇṭu). The rasa-guṇa-vīrya-vipāka set and the karma list are the attributions commonly given in dravyaguna literature for नीली and are *flagged for verification against a specific nighaṇṭu edition*, the uṣṇa vīrya especially, since the cross-system divergence claim depends on it. _Nīlībhṛṅgādi taila_ is a classical and still-manufactured keśya preparation.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tags: S core saṃhitā, N later nighaṇṭu, F folk/regional, M modern/pharmacological. No viruddha is recorded for the Chinese 青黛, which is outside the Ayurvedic canon; the classical items below belong to नीली, and the modern items to indigo naturalis as actually used today.)_

- *(N) Conditional, for नीली:* contraindicated in Pitta excess and in frank heat states, on the uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa profile; cautioned in pregnancy in the nighaṇṭu register, consistent with its anthelminthic-purgative reputation.
- *(F) Folk/regional:* Indian hair-dye use (indigo with henna) is topical and generally unproblematic, but *scalp and skin sensitization* occurs, and commercial "indigo" hair products are frequently adulterated — see the modern note below.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological — and for this drug the modern tier is the serious one:*
  - *Pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH).* The gravest signal attached to indigo naturalis. Cases of PAH have been reported in patients taking it orally for ulcerative colitis, prompting regulatory warnings in Japan. *Mechanism is not established*; onset is typically after months of use; it is at least partly reversible on withdrawal in reported cases, but not reliably so. *This is not a theoretical caution — it is the reason oral indigo naturalis is not a self-care substance.*
  - *Intussusception*, reported in the same colitis context, and *liver injury* (transaminase elevation, and more severe cases).
  - *Heavy metals.* The lime-fermentation manufacture and variable commercial quality make *lead and arsenic contamination* a real and documented concern in some indigo naturalis products. Pharmacopoeial-grade material with heavy-metal testing is not optional here.
  - *Gastrointestinal intolerance* — abdominal pain, nausea, and green-blue stool discoloration (harmless, but alarming if unexpected).
  - *Pregnancy and lactation* — insufficient data; avoid internal use.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Classical for the substance, not for this preparation — the same middle case as the Ayurveda section._ *نِيل (nīl)* is an attested drug of the Arabic materia medica, and the word is a *direct borrowing of Sanskrit* नील, which is itself the clearest evidence of the transmission route: the dye and its name reached the Arabic world from India, with the trade. Ibn al-Bayṭār treats *نيل* and *نيلج* in the *Jāmiʿ*. *But what the Arabic texts describe is Indian indigo (Indigofera), not the Chinese lime-fermented 青黛*, and the preparation, grade, and clinical register differ; this entry does not transfer Chinese indications onto the Arabic drug or the reverse. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ` for the humoral frame.

*Names:* Arabic *نِيل (nīl)*, *نِيلَج (nīlaj)*; Persian *نیل (nīl)*; Urdu *نیل*. The Andalusian and Maghrebi register also knows it as a dye through the textile trade.

*Mizāj:* classical attributions place indigo as *Cold and Dry* — commonly in the *2nd degree* — which puts it on the *same side of the thermal axis as the Chinese reading and the opposite side from the Ayurvedic one*. If the Ayurvedic uṣṇa attribution flagged above holds, then Tibb and 中醫 agree on cold while dravyaguna reads hot, which is an unusual alignment and worth recording as such. *Degree flagged for verification against a specific al-Jāmiʿ or Qānūn edition.*

*Afʿāl (actions):* *qābiḍ* (astringent); *mujaffif* (desiccant); *jālī* (cleansing); *muḥallil* (resolvent) for hot swellings; *mubarriḍ* (refrigerant) in topical application.

*Istemālāt (indications):* the register is *predominantly topical* — hot inflammatory swellings, ulcers and sores, burns, and skin complaints; also a hair and beard *dye* (*ṣābigh*), the cosmetic use that parallels both the Chinese 黛 eyebrow register and the Indian keśya one. Internal use is comparatively minor in the Arabic sources consulted.

*Mawāneʿ:* cold temperaments and cold-pattern conditions, per the Cold-Dry mizāj.

*Muṣliḥ / Badal:* no confidently attested classical pairing located for نيل specifically — *flagged rather than invented*; the class convention for Cold-Dry desiccants would be a warming aromatic adjuvant. Plausible *badal* within the actual pharmacopoeia: other cooling astringent topicals (*ṭīn armanī*, Armenian bole; *ʿafṣ*, gall).

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* *none.* Indigo is a Greco-Arabic and Indian-trade drug, not a Qurʾānic or hadith substance, and the cosmetic use of dyes in the Prophetic register concerns *henna (ḥinnāʾ)* and *katam*, not indigo. Flagged so that the dye's ubiquity in the Islamic world is not read as Prophetic provenance.#footnote[نيل / نيلج in Ibn al-Bayṭār, _Kitāb al-Jāmiʿ li-Mufradāt al-Adwiya_; the Arabic borrowing from Sanskrit नील is standard etymology (Lane, _An Arabic-English Lexicon_). Mizāj degree and the predominantly topical register are flagged for verification against a specific edition. The Prophetic hair-dye register concerns ḥinnāʾ and katam — see the hadith literature on dyeing grey hair — and not indigo; stated to forestall a spurious attribution.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

*The manufacture is the first "preparation" and is constitutive.* Fresh leaves are steeped in water until fermentation releases the indican; *slaked lime (石灰)* is added and the liquor vigorously agitated, whereupon indigo oxidizes and rises as a blue froth. *The froth is skimmed, dried, and powdered* — that skimmed foam is the true 青黛, and the classical name *靛花* ("indigo flower") names exactly it. Grade is judged by lightness: good 青黛 is a fine, light powder that *floats on water* and burns with a purple-red flame; heavy, gritty, sinking material has been bulked with lime or soil, which is the classical adulteration and the origin of the modern heavy-metal concern.

*Named formulas:*
- *黛蛤散 (Dài Gé Sǎn,* "Indigo and Clamshell Powder") — 青黛 with *蛤殼/海蛤殼* (clam shell), the signature formula for *肝火犯肺* cough with blood-streaked sputum. Taken as a powder, consistent with the no-decoction rule; often stirred into or taken alongside a decoction.
- *當歸龍薈丸 (Dāng Guī Lóng Huì Wán)* — a Liver-fire-draining pill of the Jin-Yuan tradition (attributed to Liu Wansu), containing 青黛 with 當歸, 龍膽草, 蘆薈 and other bitter-cold drugs; for exuberant Liver-Gallbladder fire with irritability, constipation, and in the classical register convulsive and manic presentations. *This formula is the historical starting point of the indirubin story* — see Modern Pharmacology. See `substances/dang-gui.typ`.
- *碧玉散 (Bì Yù Sǎn,* "Jasper Powder") — *六一散* (talc and licorice) with 青黛 added; for summerheat-damp complicated by Liver heat, with irritability and red eyes. The name records the colour the addition produces.
- *青黛散 / 青黛膏* — topical powders and ointments for mouth ulcers, throat swellings, eczema and sores; the external register is at least as important as the internal one.
- *復方青黛丸 (Fùfāng Qīng Dài Wán)* — a *modern* Chinese patent compound used for psoriasis; post-classical, flagged as such.

=== Ayurveda

*नीली* preparations belong to a separate tradition and a different plant (see above): *Nīlībhṛṅgādi taila* is the standing classical example, a medicated hair oil in the *keśya* register. There is *no Ayurvedic formula using the Chinese 青黛*.

=== Western / Greco-Roman

*Indigo was known to Greco-Roman antiquity as an imported pigment, not as a significant drug.* Dioscorides and Pliny describe *indikón* — Pliny with the characteristic confusion of a trade good whose manufacture was not understood at the receiving end — and the classical Mediterranean register is overwhelmingly *dye and paint*, with only marginal medicinal application (topical, for ulcers and inflammations). *The genuinely classical Western medicinal plant of this family is woad*, *_Isatis tinctoria_* — *glastum* to the Romans, the body-paint of Caesar's Britons and a wound-herb of the European tradition — and that story belongs to `ban-lan-gen.typ`, which treats the Greco-Roman woad register properly. *No Western tradition uses the lime-fermented Chinese pigment as a medicine.*

The later European history is economic rather than medical and is worth one line, because it explains why "indigo" is a world-historical word: Indian and later Caribbean and American indigo plantations supplied the European textile trade for centuries, until *synthetic indigo* (Baeyer, synthesis 1878; BASF industrial process from 1897) collapsed the natural-dye economy — a displacement of a crop by a laboratory that is among the clearest in the history of chemistry.

=== Tibb (Arabic-Islamic)

Covered above: *نيل* as a predominantly *topical* Cold-Dry astringent and desiccant for hot swellings and skin complaints, plus the hair-dye register. No compound *murakkab* built specifically around it has been identified in the sources consulted here.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

_This section is necessarily brief and is adjusted to what the substance is. 青黛 is neither a food nor a climate-tied dietary article: it is a manufactured pigment taken in small medicinal doses by indication. What the climate frame genuinely illuminates here is its *production*, which is warmth-dependent, and the fact that the drug's heat-clearing indication is commonest in the climate where the plants grow._ See `climates.typ`.

=== Warm-humid subtropical East and Southeast Asia (the production zone)

Köppen Cfa/Cwa/Am — Fujian, Guangdong, Guangxi, Yunnan, Taiwan, Vietnam, and for the knotweed, Japan. *The fermentation is the climatic constraint*: indican release depends on microbial activity in a steeping vat, which requires sustained warmth, and the traditional calendar harvests leaves in summer and ferments immediately. This is why indigo manufacture is a warm-season, warm-region craft everywhere it occurs, from Tokushima to Bengal to the Caribbean.

*Constitutionally*, the same zone carries *濕熱* (damp-heat) with the Wen Bing warm-disease presentations — macular eruptions, throat and mouth heat-toxin, summer convulsive fevers in children — that are precisely 青黛's indications. The plant of the hot damp region treats the pathology of the hot damp region, which is the same unremarkable-but-real pattern noted for coix and poria: a pharmacopoeia develops its heat-clearing drugs from its own flora.

=== The Japanese craft register (a non-medical note)

The Tokushima *阿波藍* tradition composts the knotweed leaves into *sukumo* over months before vat-fermentation — a more elaborate process than the Chinese lime method and aimed at *cloth*, not medicine. Noted because Japanese *seitai* as a drug and Japanese *ai* as a dye come from the same plant and the same region but are different products of different processes; conflating them overstates the medical tradition.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_ / _kāla-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26)

The meaningful transposition for this drug is not geographic but *of form and context*. Indigo naturalis has moved from a powdered drug used in short courses by prescription into *long-term daily oral self-administration* for inflammatory bowel disease, often from unregulated sources — and the serious harms reported (pulmonary hypertension, liver injury) have arisen in exactly that register, not in classical short-course use. A drug the tradition warned against taking for long periods internally is now, in some hands, taken for months. That is a *mātrā*- and *kāla*-order error rather than a *deśa* one, and it is the single most important practical thing in this entry.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Not a nutritive substance; no per-100 g profile applies. The pharmacology is unusually consequential for a drug of this size.

*Key constituents:*
- *Indirubin (靛玉紅)* — the red isomer of indigo, present at low percentage; *the component with the most significant modern history*. A *cyclin-dependent kinase (CDK) inhibitor* and an *aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AhR) agonist*.
- *Indigotin (indigo)* — the blue pigment and the bulk of the material; poorly absorbed.
- *Tryptanthrin* — anti-inflammatory and antimicrobial in preclinical work; also found in the sibling drugs.
- *Isatin*, *qingdainone*, β-sitosterol; variable *calcium carbonate / lime* residue from manufacture, which is why ash content is a grade criterion.

*The indirubin → CML story — one of the two best-documented classical-formula-to-modern-drug translations in Chinese medicine, alongside artemisinin.* In the 1970s Chinese investigators studying *當歸龍薈丸* for chronic myelogenous leukemia traced the activity to 青黛 and then to *indirubin*. Indirubin itself is poorly soluble and poorly absorbed, so derivatives were developed — *meisoindigo (甲異靛)* became a clinically used agent for CML in China. The mechanistic account that followed (CDK inhibition; later, the broader indirubin-derivative literature in kinase pharmacology) is a genuine contribution from the Chinese materia medica to kinase-targeted oncology. *Proper framing:* this is a *classical formula leading chemists to a molecule*, not evidence that the classical formula treats leukemia as given. See `ban-lan-gen.typ`, which notes the same translation from the root drug's side.

*Ulcerative colitis — good evidence and a serious harm, which must be stated together.*
- *Efficacy:* randomized placebo-controlled trial evidence from Japan (the INDIGO study and related trials) found oral indigo naturalis significantly more effective than placebo for inducing clinical response and mucosal healing in active ulcerative colitis, with effect sizes that compare respectably to conventional agents. The proposed mechanism is *AhR activation* by indirubin and related indoles, driving IL-22 production and mucosal repair — a coherent story linking the constituent, the receptor, and the outcome.
- *Harm:* the same programme surfaced *pulmonary arterial hypertension*, together with *intussusception* and *liver injury*. Japanese authorities issued warnings and trials were affected. PAH is a serious, potentially irreversible condition, and its appearance after months of use in otherwise young patients is the reason indigo naturalis for colitis *is not a self-treatment* and should only ever proceed with specialist supervision and monitoring.
- *The honest summary:* this is a case where a traditional drug has real, trial-demonstrated efficacy for a modern indication *and* a real, serious, idiosyncratic toxicity — and where the popular framing ("natural remedy works for colitis!") omits precisely the half that matters most.

*Psoriasis — the better-behaved indication.* *Topical* indigo naturalis ointment has reasonable randomized evidence, largely from Taiwan, for plaque psoriasis and for nail psoriasis, with the obvious cosmetic drawback of staining. Topical use does not carry the systemic risks above, which makes the risk-benefit far more favourable than for oral use.

*Other:* antimicrobial, anti-inflammatory, and antiviral activity in preclinical models; the antiviral claims attaching to this drug family in the SARS/COVID public register are treated in `ban-lan-gen.typ` and are *not* supported for 青黛 specifically.

*Safe dosage / upper limit:*
- *Internal (classical and pharmacopoeial):* *1.5–3 g/day*, as powder in pills or capsules or stirred into strained decoction — *never decocted*, being insoluble. Short courses.
- *Oral indigo naturalis in the colitis trials* used higher doses (on the order of 0.5–2 g/day of standardized material) over months; *that register is investigational and specialist-supervised*, not an extension of classical dosing.
- *External:* as powder or ointment, no fixed limit; staining is the practical constraint.
- *Contraindicated / avoid:* Spleen-Stomach vacuity cold and cold-pattern conditions; pregnancy and lactation; *prolonged internal self-administration* in any context.
- *Monitoring* for anyone on sustained oral use: liver enzymes, and clinical vigilance for *exertional dyspnoea* as the presenting symptom of PAH.
- *Source quality is a safety matter*, not just an efficacy one: insist on pharmacopoeial-grade material with heavy-metal testing.
- *Reference thresholds, not personalized medical advice; the oral colitis use in particular belongs with a specialist.*#footnote[Constituents and the indirubin → meisoindigo translation: Chinese pharmacological literature from the 1970s onward on 當歸龍薈丸, 青黛 and indirubin; indirubin as CDK inhibitor and AhR agonist is well characterized in the kinase-pharmacology literature. Ulcerative-colitis efficacy: Naganuma et al. and the Japanese randomized trial programme (the INDIGO study), published in the gastroenterology literature from ~2018 — verify against current systematic reviews. Pulmonary arterial hypertension, intussusception and hepatotoxicity associated with oral indigo naturalis: Japanese case reports and regulatory safety communications from the mid-2010s onward; mechanism unestablished. Topical indigo naturalis for plaque and nail psoriasis: randomized trials principally from Taiwan (Lin and colleagues). Heavy-metal contamination of indigo naturalis products: pharmacopoeial quality literature. Doses: _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ 青黛 monograph and Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. All modern clinical and regulatory claims require current verification.]

